    details: `The network is designed for deterministic latency and zero packet loss — both critical for electronic trading where a single dropped packet could mean a missed opportunity or incorrect risk assessment. The spine-leaf topology in the trading network ensures any server can reach any other server with exactly 2 hops (source leaf → spine → destination leaf), providing predictable ~3-5µs switching latency. All trading traffic flows on dedicated VLANs with CoS (Class of Service) marking — market data traffic gets strict priority queueing (CoS 5), order traffic gets high priority (CoS 4), and management traffic is best-effort (CoS 0). PFC (Priority Flow Control) enables lossless Ethernet for RDMA over Converged Ethernet (RoCEv2), allowing trading engines to use RDMA for inter-server data transfer with < 5µs latency and CPU bypass. Broker connectivity is engineered for maximum reliability: 4 dedicated leased lines to NSE co-location at BKC Mumbai provide 4 independent paths with sub-millisecond RTT. BGP routing selects the primary path based on latency measurements; on failure, traffic fails over to the next best path within < 1 second thanks to BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection). For brokers not in NSE co-location, site-to-site IPSec VPN tunnels with redundant endpoints provide secure connectivity. WAN connectivity uses dark fiber (Mumbai to Pune) with DWDM multiplexing providing 32x100Gbps waves — far exceeding current requirements but ensuring 10-year scalability. The SD-WAN overlay provides application-aware routing: market data replication gets the lowest-latency path, database replication gets the highest-bandwidth path, and management traffic uses the most cost-effective path. Network security implements a zero-trust model: even within the trading VLAN, services must authenticate to communicate. Rakshak issues mTLS certificates to every service instance (rotation every 24 hours), and the service mesh enforces mutual TLS for all gRPC and REST communications. Network access control (NAC) via Cisco ISE ensures only authenticated, compliant devices can connect to any network port — 802.1X with certificate-based authentication for user devices, MAC authentication bypass for infrastructure devices.`,
