    details: `The database architecture is designed around the distinct data patterns of algorithmic trading. PostgreSQL handles transactional workloads: order tracking (order_id, client_id, strategy_id, broker, symbol, quantity, price, order_type, order_status, timestamps), trade records (trade_id, order_id, execution_price, executed_quantity, broker_trade_id, timestamps), position snapshots (account_id, symbol, net_quantity, average_price, realized_pnl, unrealized_pnl, timestamp), user and account management (users, roles, permissions, api_keys, broker_accounts), and strategy configurations (strategy_id, parameters, constraints, risk_limits, deployment_status). All tables use UUID primary keys for distributed uniqueness and are partitioned by month on timestamp columns for efficient data management and query performance. PostgreSQL synchronous replication ensures zero data loss: every committed transaction is replicated to the Pune standby before acknowledging to the application. ClickHouse handles analytical workloads: market data (ticks with nanosecond timestamps from TalkOptions, OHLC bars from Ganesh across 15 timeframes, order book snapshots every 50ms), strategy performance analytics (daily P&L per strategy, attribution analysis, execution quality metrics), and audit log analytics (order latency percentiles, error rates, risk breach patterns). ClickHouse uses the ReplicatedMergeTree engine with data sharded by symbol hash and replicated across both nodes. Compression achieves 10:1 ratio on tick data due to columnar storage and delta encoding of timestamps. Redis provides sub-millisecond access to shared state: position cache (updated by Vega on every trade, read by Suraksha for risk checks, by VYUH for strategy decisions, by TalkOffice for dashboard display), order book cache (top 5 bid/ask levels for 5,000+ symbols, updated via TalkOptions real-time feed, used by Delta XI for microstructure analysis), rate limiting counters (per-API-key, per-IP counters with token bucket algorithm, enforced by TalkOptions and Narad), session state (user sessions for TradePilot, TalkOffice, DXCC with 30-minute TTL), and ML feature store (latest feature values for TalkDelta AI models, populated by feature computation pipelines). The Redis Enterprise cluster uses Active-Active replication with CRDTs between Mumbai and Pune, providing local latency in both sites. MinIO provides S3-compatible object storage for immutable data: database backups (daily full backups + continuous WAL archiving), regulatory archives (7-year retention of all trade records, order logs, audit trails per SEBI requirements), and application artifacts (strategy backtest reports, model training datasets, historical market data snapshots). All data is encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM) and in transit (TLS 1.3). Backup verification runs monthly — a random point-in-time is selected, the backup is restored to a sandbox PostgreSQL, and data integrity checks validate record counts and checksums against production.`,
