    details: `The API architecture follows the "API Gateway" pattern with Kong serving as the central ingress point for all API traffic. Kong provides a plugin-based architecture enabling consistent application of cross-cutting concerns: (1) Authentication — validates API keys (external consumers) or JWT tokens (internal services), rejects unauthenticated requests with 401, (2) Rate Limiting — token bucket per API key, configured per endpoint based on tier, returns 429 with Retry-After header when exceeded, (3) Request Transformation — normalizes incoming requests to internal format, adds correlation IDs, logs request metadata, (4) Response Transformation — normalizes outgoing responses (envelope format: { status, data, meta, errors }), adds CORS headers, compresses payloads (gzip/brotli), (5) Circuit Breaking — if a backend service exceeds error threshold (50% over 30 seconds), circuit opens and requests immediately return 503 with cached/stale response if available, (6) Logging — every API call generates a structured log entry (JSON) to ClickHouse for analytics and MQ for real-time monitoring. API versioning strategy: major versions are indicated in the URL path (/v1/resource, /v2/resource). Minor versions (additive changes) are transparent within a major version. The version lifecycle provides predictability: new versions are in "Current" state for active development, transition to "Supported" when a newer version is released, enter "Deprecated" with 12-month notice (returning Deprecation and Sunset HTTP headers), and finally "Retired" after 12 months. The API Gateway enforces this by rejecting requests to retired versions with 410 Gone. Authentication flow: external consumers obtain an API key via the developer portal (self-service with email verification). API keys are 256-bit random tokens stored hashed (SHA-256) in Rakshak. Each key has: (1) scopes — specific API endpoints or endpoint groups accessible, (2) rate limit tier — basic/pro/enterprise, (3) expiry — optional expiration date, (4) IP whitelist — optional CIDR restriction. API keys are passed in the X-API-Key header. Internal services authenticate via OAuth2 client credentials flow, obtaining short-lived JWT tokens (15-minute expiry) from Rakshak. JWT tokens include: service identity, allowed scopes, token ID (for revocation). Additionally, service-to-service communication uses mTLS with certificates issued by Rakshak's internal CA, rotated every 24 hours. SDK generation: OpenAPI 3.0 specifications for all APIs serve as the source of truth. CI/CD pipelines auto-generate client SDKs in Python, JavaScript/TypeScript, C++, Java, and Go using OpenAPI Generator. SDKs are published to internal package registries (PyPI mirror, npm registry, Maven repository) and include: typed API methods, request/response models, authentication handling, retry logic, connection pooling, and usage examples.`,
