    details: `The white-label architecture enables financial services firms (brokers, wealth managers, fintech platforms) to offer Algo IQ's algorithmic trading capabilities under their own brand. The system implements multi-tenancy with strong isolation while sharing infrastructure for cost efficiency. Tenant identification: every API request includes a tenant claim (derived from API key or JWT token). The Tenant Manager validates the tenant status (active/suspended/terminated) and enriches the request context with tenant_id and tenant_config (tier, feature flags, rate limits). This tenant context propagates through all service calls via gRPC metadata or MQ message headers. Database isolation uses PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS) as the primary mechanism. All tables include a tenant_id column (UUID). RLS policies are automatically applied per-query: ALTER TABLE orders ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY; CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON orders USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant_id')::UUID); The current tenant is set at connection acquisition from the pool via pgBouncer using SET app.current_tenant_id = '{tenant_id}'. For premium-tier partners requiring additional isolation (regulatory requirement, or partner preference), separate PostgreSQL schemas can be provisioned — tenant_a.orders, tenant_b.orders — with schema-level access control. MQ namespace isolation is achieved through topic prefixing. MQ topics follow the pattern: {tenantId}.{domain}.{entity}.{action}. For example: "tenant-abc.strategy.STRAT-001.signal", "tenant-abc.order.ORDER-001.filled". MQ authorization rules enforce that consumers (identified by their mTLS certificate with tenant claim) can only subscribe to topics matching their tenant prefix. Cross-tenant subscription attempts are rejected and logged as security events by Rakshak. White-label applications (TradePilot, TalkOffice) are deployed as containerized instances per partner, configured at startup with tenant-specific branding and API endpoints. The branding injection happens via environment variables: APP_TENANT_ID, APP_BRAND_NAME, APP_BRAND_LOGO_URL, APP_BRAND_COLOR_PRIMARY, APP_BRAND_DOMAIN. The application shell (React/Electron) reads these at startup and applies them to the UI without code changes. Each tenant gets a dedicated domain (partner.algotrading.com) or can use their own domain with CNAME pointing to the Algo IQ white-label gateway. The Partner Admin Portal provides complete self-service: (1) Client Management — create client accounts, manage KYC documents, map broker accounts, set risk limits per client, (2) Strategy Management — assign strategies from the Algo IQ catalog to clients, configure strategy parameters within allowed ranges, monitor client strategy performance, (3) Branding — upload brand assets (logo, favicon), configure color scheme (primary, secondary, accent), customize email templates, set domain/DNS, (4) User Management — create partner staff accounts (admin, risk_manager, support) with role-based permissions, (5) Billing — view real-time usage (strategies active, orders placed, API calls made), download monthly invoices, configure payment method (credit card, net banking, UPI), set billing alerts, (6) Compliance — generate SEBI-format trade logs (date range, client filter), export position statements, download P&L reports, access audit trail for dispute resolution. Billing is metered across multiple dimensions: (1) Strategy-hour — each active strategy per hour, (2) Order — each order placed (regardless of fill), (3) API Call — each API request to TalkOptions or other data APIs, (4) Data Volume — GB of market data consumed, (5) Storage — GB of historical data retained beyond default period. Usage is aggregated by Chitragupta in real-time with hourly rollups to a billing database. Monthly invoices are generated automatically on the 1st of each month with GST-compliant format (invoice number, GSTIN, SAC code, taxable value, CGST, SGST/IGST, total).`,
