    details: `The deployment architecture enables rapid, safe delivery of changes to the ecosystem with minimal risk of service disruption. The CI/CD pipeline follows a strict progression: (1) Developer pushes to feature branch → (2) CI runs build + unit tests + linting → (3) Developer creates merge request to develop → (4) CI runs full test suite (unit + integration + security scans) → (5) Code review required (at least 2 approvals) → (6) Merge to develop → (7) CI builds and deploys to staging environment → (8) Automated smoke tests verify critical paths (place order → receive fill → update position → verify P&L) → (9) Merge request to main (release) → (10) CI builds production artifacts, security scans, signs artifacts → (11) CD deploys using canary (10% traffic → monitor 30 min → 50% → monitor 30 min → 100%) → (12) Post-deployment monitoring for 2 hours with automated rollback trigger. Build optimizations for C++ trading engines: ccache distributed across CI runners (Redis backend) provides 90%+ cache hit rate for incremental builds, reducing build time from 45 minutes to 3 minutes. LTO (Link-Time Optimization) with -O3 -march=native produces optimized binaries achieving the required < 500µs latency. PGO (Profile-Guided Optimization) uses production profile data to further optimize branch prediction and cache behavior. Binary signing with Sigstore/Cosign ensures supply chain integrity — each binary is signed with a keyless certificate linked to the Git commit SHA, providing verifiable provenance from source code to deployed binary. Database migration strategy follows expand-contract pattern for zero-downtime schema changes: (1) Expand — add new columns/tables (backward-compatible, old code ignores them), deploy application code that writes to both old and new schema, (2) Migrate — background migration copies existing data to new schema, (3) Contract — deploy application code that reads only from new schema, (4) Cleanup — remove old columns/tables in a separate deployment. This ensures no deployment requires downtime. Environment promotion model: (1) Dev — per-developer environment (Kubernetes namespace with subset of services, mock brokers). Developers deploy at will for testing. (2) Staging — shared integration environment with full ecosystem, simulated broker connections, and production-like market data replay. All develop-branch merges auto-deploy here. (3) Production — three-site deployment managed via GitOps. Only release-branch merges (after full testing in staging) promote to production. Canary deployment details: (1) Deploy new version to 10% of instances (1 of 10 replicas for containerized, 1 of 3 servers for bare-metal), (2) Route 10% of orders/traffic to new version using Consul service mesh traffic splitting, (3) Monitor for 30 minutes: error rates, latency p95, order fill rates, and risk metrics, (4) If all metrics within normal bounds, increase to 50% and monitor 30 minutes, (5) If stable, promote to 100%. If any metric exceeds threshold at any stage, automated rollback: ArgoCD syncs to previous Git revision (containerized) or Ansible runs rollback playbook (bare-metal), traffic shifts back to old version. Deployment verification uses a "smoke test" suite that simulates a complete trade lifecycle: create strategy order → risk check approval (Suraksha) → order placed (Vega) → simulated fill (narad mock broker) → position update (Vega) → P&L calculation (Chitragupta) → dashboard update (TalkOffice). This test runs automatically after every deployment and validates end-to-end system integrity.`,
