    details: `The High Availability architecture is designed for 99.999% uptime (5 minutes 15 seconds of downtime per year). This requires: (1) no single points of failure at any layer, (2) automated failure detection and remediation faster than human response time, (3) geographic redundancy to survive site-level disasters, and (4) rigorous testing to validate failover mechanisms. Service redundancy model: each service is classified as Critical (trading path), Essential (analytics, data), or Operational (dashboards, reporting). Critical services (Vega, VYUH, Suraksha, MQ, Narad) have N+1 within-site redundancy plus hot standby at DR-1. The primary instance actively processes, with state continuously replicated. On failure, the standby promotes to primary — for Vega this involves: (1) Kavach detects primary Vega missing heartbeats (quorum decision), (2) Failover Orchestrator verifies standby is healthy and has received all replicated state (order state, position data), (3) Standby promoted — takes over MQ topic leadership, starts processing orders, (4) Consul updated with new primary address, (5) Clients transparently reconnect (MQ clients on reconnect, REST clients via load balancer). Total failover time: < 2 seconds for MQ-based clients, < 10 seconds for REST clients (due to DNS TTL). Database HA uses PostgreSQL Patroni with etcd distributed consensus: (1) 3 PostgreSQL nodes, one elected as primary by etcd (with etcd cluster also 3 nodes across sites), (2) Mumbai node is primary, Pune node is synchronous standby (synchronous_commit = remote_write), Hyderabad node is asynchronous standby, (3) On primary failure (Mumbai), etcd detects via Patroni health checks (every 5 seconds), (4) etcd triggers leader election — Pune standby (most up-to-date) is elected new primary, (5) Patroni promotes Pune to primary, updates etcd with new leader, (6) Application reconnects via pgBouncer which queries Patroni REST API for current primary. Failover time: < 30 seconds. Zero data loss because synchronous standby was caught up at failure moment. MQ HA uses a clustered broker architecture: (1) Each site has 3 MQ broker nodes forming a cluster for that site's topics, (2) Cross-site: topic shards are replicated between sites — producer in Mumbai publishes to local MQ, which replicates to Pune MQ cluster, (3) Broker nodes use Raft for topic leader election — if the leader fails, a follower is elected within < 1 second, (4) Producers and consumers discover available brokers via Consul and automatically reconnect to the new leader. Heartbeat-based health monitoring: every service publishes a heartbeat to MQ topic "system.health.{serviceId}.{instanceId}" every 100ms. The heartbeat message includes: service ID, instance ID, status (OK/DEGRADED), metrics (CPU%, memory%, latency p95, error rate, queue depth), and timestamp. Kavach monitoring nodes consume ALL heartbeat topics and maintain per-instance state: (1) Healthy — heartbeats arriving within 150ms of expected interval, metrics within normal bounds, (2) Degraded — heartbeats delayed (150-300ms) OR metrics exceeding warning thresholds, (3) Unhealthy — heartbeats missing > 300ms OR metrics exceeding critical thresholds. Health decisions are made by Raft consensus across the 3 Kavach nodes to prevent split-brain (e.g., network partition between sites should not cause both sites to think the other is down). A service is marked DOWN only if 2 of 3 Kavach nodes agree it is unhealthy. This prevents a single monitoring node failure from triggering false failovers. Site-level failover is a manual decision (automating full-site failover risks split-brain scenarios where both sites think they are primary, leading to duplicate orders). The runbook defines: (1) Operations Lead declares site emergency based on: multiple service failures, network isolation, facility issue (power, cooling), (2) Before failover: verify DR site readiness (services healthy, data replication caught up, broker connectivity active), (3) Execute failover: shift DNS to DR site, update broker connections to DR site IPs (coordinate with broker operations teams), verify order flow through DR site, (4) Monitor DR site for 30 minutes before declaring stable, (5) After primary site restored: reverse replication (DR → primary), controlled failback during market-off hours. Regular DR testing validates: (1) Service failover — every quarter, a random critical service is failed over to DR-1 and runs from DR for 24 hours, (2) Database failover — every quarter, PostgreSQL primary role is switched to DR site and runs for 24 hours, (3) Full site failover — annually, all trading is cut over to DR-1 and run for 1 week from DR site. All tests are conducted during live market hours (initially with simulated orders, then with small real positions) to validate real-world failover behavior.`,
