    details: `The disaster recovery architecture addresses the full spectrum of failure scenarios, from single-component failure (covered by HA architecture) to complete site loss. DR planning follows the NIST SP 800-34 framework: (1) Business Impact Analysis — identifies critical business functions (trading execution, risk management, regulatory reporting) and their maximum tolerable downtime (MTD), (2) Recovery Strategy — defines how each function will be recovered (hot standby, warm standby, cold archive) based on MTD, (3) Implementation — builds the DR infrastructure, documents runbooks, trains personnel, (4) Testing — validates recovery procedures through regular drills, (5) Maintenance — keeps DR plans current as the ecosystem evolves. The 3-tier recovery hierarchy provides graduated recovery capabilities: (1) Tier 1 (Hot Standby) handles single-component failures and minor incidents. This is the HA architecture operating within its designed scope. Automated failover provides sub-minute recovery with zero data loss. The majority of incidents (estimated 95%+) are handled at this tier without human intervention. (2) Tier 2 (Warm Standby) handles single-site loss (primary site unavailable due to facility failure, network isolation, or regional disaster). This requires human decision (operations lead declares site emergency) followed by runbook execution. The 4-hour RTO accounts for: decision time (30 min), service startup at DR-2 (30 min), data synchronization verification (30 min), integration testing (1 hour), broker/exchange coordination for connectivity cutover (1 hour), and full trading resumption (30 min). (3) Tier 3 (Cold Archive) handles catastrophic scenarios (dual-site loss, ransomware encrypting all live data, data corruption propagating through replication). This relies on immutable backups stored in AWS S3 Glacier Deep Archive. The 72-hour RTO accounts for: backup identification and integrity verification (4 hours), data transfer from Glacier (12-48 hours depending on dataset size, expedited retrieval used), infrastructure provisioning (8 hours, using Infrastructure as Code to rebuild environment), data restoration (12 hours for multi-TB databases), application deployment and configuration (8 hours), integration testing (12 hours), and trading resumption. Backup architecture implements defense-in-depth against data loss: (1) Rubrik appliances at each site manage backup policies, (2) Local snapshots provide instant recovery for recent data (30-day retention), (3) Replicated to MinIO at DR sites for medium-term retention (1 year), (4) Archived to S3 Glacier for long-term regulatory retention (7 years). All backup data is encrypted (AES-256-GCM) with keys managed in HashiCorp Vault and replicated to a secure off-site key repository. Immutability is enforced at multiple levels: (1) Rubrik logical air gap — backups stored with retention lock, cannot be deleted or modified before expiry even by administrators, (2) MinIO object lock (governance mode) — objects cannot be deleted before retention period, requires special privilege to override, (3) S3 Glacier Vault Lock — compliance mode with "write-once-read-many" (WORM) policy, absolutely immutable once locked. Ransomware defense: backup infrastructure uses a completely separate authentication domain (no Active Directory integration), separate network segment with strict firewall rules (backup servers can initiate connections to production for backup, production cannot initiate connections to backup infrastructure), separate administrative credentials stored in a break-glass password vault (physically separate from normal password management), and regular "delete attempt" testing — designated testers attempt to delete backup data, verifying that immutability controls prevent it. Recovery runbooks are detailed, step-by-step procedures executed via Rundeck automation: Runbook: "PostgreSQL Recovery" includes steps: (1) Verify latest valid backup (Rubrik API check), (2) Provision new PostgreSQL server (Terraform apply with DR-specific variables), (3) Restore base backup (pg_basebackup from Rubrik), (4) Apply WAL segments to reach desired recovery point (PITR), (5) Start PostgreSQL and verify data integrity (row counts, checksums), (6) Update pgBouncer configuration to point to new primary, (7) Verify application connectivity and data access, (8) Notify operations of completion. Runbook: "Full Site Failover to Pune" includes: (1) Verify Pune DR-1 readiness (all services healthy, data replication current), (2) Stop new order acceptance at Mumbai (prevent split-brain), (3) Wait for replication lag to reach zero (confirm all transactions replicated), (4) Promote Pune PostgreSQL to primary (Patroni manual promotion), (5) Activate Pune MQ cluster as primary (update Consul service registry), (6) Start Pune application instances (Vega, VYUH, Suraksha), (7) Update broker connectivity (notify brokers of new IP addresses/endpoints), (8) Verify order flow (place test order through full path), (9) Update DNS for external access (TradePilot, TalkOffice), (10) Notify all stakeholders of site transition. Recovery testing validates both the procedures and the infrastructure: (1) Weekly — automated restore verification: randomly selected backup file restored to sandbox, data integrity checks run, result logged to monitoring, (2) Monthly — service-level recovery test: randomly selected service (e.g., Chitragupta) restored from backup to sandbox, verified it can process historical data and produce correct output, (3) Quarterly — DR-1 live failover: critical services failed over to Pune DR-1, run from Pune for 24 hours with live trading, verifying that real-world failover performs as expected, (4) Annually — Full DR-2 activation: complete ecosystem rebuilt at Hyderabad DR-2 from backups, run for 1 week with simulated trading, verifying comprehensive recovery capability. Post-test analysis identifies gaps and drives improvements: runbook steps that were unclear, assumptions that proved wrong, time estimates that were inaccurate, dependencies that were undocumented. All findings are tracked in Jira with remediation plans completed within 30 days.`,
